Closes CNA-FINDINGS A6 -- Parallel::For creates one operating-system thread per iteration.
AGAINST ../../sharp-runtimenext, not cnanext.

WHAT IS WRONG
  Parallel::For launches one std::async per index and waits every maxDeg of them. The batching
  bounds how many threads exist at once -- which is what fixed the 50 000-thread crash the comment
  above it describes -- but not how many are *created*: a loop of N iterations still creates N
  threads, N/maxDeg batches deep, and libstdc++'s std::async does not pool.

  For a loop that runs once over a large collection the creation cost amortises away, and it is
  the right tool: cna-city uses it exactly there, to generate a hundred thousand citizens. For a
  loop running thirty times a second it is thousands of thread creations per second and the
  creations cost more than the work -- which is why cna-city's tick has a persistent pool of its
  own rather than using this.

WHAT THIS CHANGES
  One launch per *worker* over a contiguous range, which is what .NET's TPL does. Thread creations
  drop from N to min(maxDeg, N) per call. The public API does not change.

  Contracts: iterations of a Parallel::For have never been ordered or isolated from each other, so
  partitioning takes nothing away. An exception still abandons the rest of its own partition and
  surfaces in the AggregateException -- the partition is larger than one iteration now, which
  matches .NET, where a partition is also abandoned as a unit.

STATE OF THIS PATCH -- READ THIS ONE BEFORE APPLYING
  Applies cleanly, and a consumer translation unit that calls Parallel::For compiles cleanly
  against it. It has NOT been run, and unlike the two cnanext patches beside it this one changes
  *behaviour* rather than filling in a missing call: it must go through sharp-runtime's own
  threading-tasks test suite before it lands. In particular the ParallelLoopState overloads and
  both ForEach variants are NOT touched here and still launch per item; they want the same
  treatment and a careful look at what Break and Stop should mean across a partition.

  A useful end-to-end check afterwards, because it exercises the changed path over a hundred
  thousand items and compares the result bit for bit:

      cd ../cna-city && cmake --build build -j$(nproc)
      ./build/cna-city --seed 42 --agents 100000 --simulate 2h --checksum

  The six digests must be unchanged from before the patch, and the two "reproduced" lines must both
  say yes. Population generation runs through Parallel::For, so a partitioning that changed which
  citizen got which home would move CITY and AGENTS immediately.

--- a/modules/threading-tasks/include/System/Threading/Tasks/Parallel.hpp	2026-08-19 06:56:09.704570360 +0200
+++ b/modules/threading-tasks/include/System/Threading/Tasks/Parallel.hpp	2026-09-03 20:19:02.771457461 +0200
@@ -276,11 +276,34 @@
 
             std::vector<std::future<void>> futures;
             std::vector<std::exception_ptr> exceptions;
-            for (intcs i = fromInclusive; i < toExclusive; ++i) {
-                futures.push_back(std::async(std::launch::async, [body, i]{ body(i); }));
-                if (static_cast<intcs>(futures.size()) >= maxDeg) {
-                    waitAllCollectingExceptions(futures, exceptions);
-                    futures.clear();
+
+            // One launch per *worker*, over a contiguous range, rather than one per iteration.
+            //
+            // Batching by maxDeg bounds how many threads exist at once, which is what stopped the
+            // 50 000-thread crash described above -- but it does not bound how many are *created*:
+            // a loop of N iterations still creates N of them, N/maxDeg batches deep, and
+            // libstdc++'s std::async does not pool. For a loop that runs once over a large
+            // collection the creation cost amortises away. For a loop that runs thirty times a
+            // second it is thousands of thread creations per second, and the creations cost more
+            // than the work they carry -- measured by CNA City, which uses this for population
+            // generation (where it is the right tool) and had to write its own persistent pool for
+            // the tick (where it was not).
+            //
+            // Partitioning is also what real .NET does, and it changes no contract that was being
+            // relied on: the iterations of a Parallel::For have never been ordered or isolated
+            // from each other, and an exception still abandons the rest of its own partition and
+            // surfaces in the AggregateException.
+            const intcs total = toExclusive - fromInclusive;
+            if (total > 0) {
+                const intcs workers = maxDeg < total ? maxDeg : total;
+                const intcs chunk = (total + workers - 1) / workers;
+                for (intcs w = 0; w < workers; ++w) {
+                    const intcs begin = fromInclusive + w * chunk;
+                    if (begin >= toExclusive) break;
+                    const intcs end = (begin + chunk < toExclusive) ? begin + chunk : toExclusive;
+                    futures.push_back(std::async(std::launch::async, [body, begin, end]{
+                        for (intcs i = begin; i < end; ++i) body(i);
+                    }));
                 }
             }
             waitAllCollectingExceptions(futures, exceptions);
